            <div xml:id="d420">

                <pb facs="0476" n="426" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg426"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Gresham to Mr.
                            Gray.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-01-12"><hi rendition="#italic">January 12,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 167.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received Mr. Butler’s dispatch of
                    the 20th ultimo, No. 183, giving a copy of his testimony, at the instance of the
                    second civil judge of first instance, in the suit of Marcial Yslas <hi
                        rendition="#italic">vs</hi>. The St. Louis and Zacatecas Ore Company. The
                    subject upon which he was called to testify was purely official, he being asked
                    whether he knew, and whether it was of record, that the Congress of the United
                    States had enacted a tariff law on October 1, 1890, by which 30 per cent duty
                    was levied on the importation of metals. And he was further asked how and why he
                    knew it. Mr. Butler asks approval of his action.</p>
                <p>It is a well established rule that no public minister can testify in a civil or
                    criminal case without the authorization of his Government. Moreover, he can not
                    even testify as a private individual, for he may not waive his official
                    character and immunities without express authorization of his Government.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. Q. Gresham.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
